data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**A1: Keep carts in Redis or Postgres?** Carts have outgrown the 4 KB session cookie, so they need a home on the server. The cookie would keep only a cart ID, and the cart itself would live in one of these two stores. No `explain` skill is available in this session, so I explained it directly.\n\n**Example:** a shopper has 30 items in their cart. Each item's name, price and image link adds up to more than 4 KB, so the browser can't hold the cart anymore.\n\n**Option a1: Redis**\n- Carts live in memory and expire on their own after a set time, such as 14 days.\n- Reads are very fast.\n- If Redis restarts, or evicts keys when memory runs low, carts can disappear. Shoppers lose their items.\n- Reporting on carts, such as finding abandoned carts, is harder.\n- If Redis isn't already in the stack, it's one more service to run.\n\n**Option a2: Postgres (the main agent's recommendation)**\n- Carts are rows in a table, so they survive restarts.\n- Checkout can read the cart and create the order in one transaction.\n- Abandoned carts are easy to query.\n- Reads are a little slower than Redis, which is unlikely to matter at cart volumes.\n- It needs a cleanup job to delete old carts.\n\n**My recommendation:** Postgres, unless the team already runs Redis and accepts occasionally losing carts. Losing a shopper's cart costs sales, and Postgres avoids that risk with less new infrastructure.\n\nI haven't changed anything in the main agent's checkout. A1 is still open, and you can answer it with its buttons in this chat's Asks. Tell me which option you pick and I'll resolve A1 with a note for the main agent."}}

